{"created": "2026-07-03T22:19:39.778797+00:00", "modified": "2026-07-07T13:42:30.443104+00:00", "id": 21181978, "conceptrecid": "21181977", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.21181978", "conceptdoi": "10.5281/zenodo.21181977", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21181978", "metadata": {"title": "Target-Edge Leakage Inflates Topological Link Prediction: A Quantification and a Corrected Protocol", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.21181978", "publication_date": "2026-07-04", "description": "<p class=\"FirstParagraph\">Persistent homology is increasingly used to build node and neighborhood features for graph learning. When those features are computed once on the full graph and then reused to predict individual edges, the feature of a node already encodes the very edge being predicted, and the reported accuracy is inflated. I quantify this target-edge leakage for topological link prediction across ten graphs, six real, including citation, coauthorship, and literary networks, and four synthetic, spanning scale-free, community, small-world, and spatial topologies. Under a protocol that removes each fold&rsquo;s test edges from the graph before any persistence feature is computed, apparent performance falls sharply and systematically: the mean inflation of the naive protocol is 0.11 AUC, reaches 0.20 on the citation graph, and on several graphs drops corrected single-parameter performance to chance, with a 95 percent confidence interval on the inflation that excludes zero. The inflation is present on all ten graphs and for both single-parameter and multi-parameter features, and on a majority of graphs it reverses which of two methods appears better. As a controlled application of the protocol I compare single-parameter and multi-parameter fibered persistence, and find that once leakage is removed the multi-parameter advantage for edge prediction is small but statistically significant across the ten graphs, on the order of 0.01 to 0.03 AUC and concentrated where single-parameter persistence is weak. The reported performance of topological link prediction depends more on whether the protocol controls target-edge leakage than on the choice of persistence invariant, and I recommend the corrected protocol as a default for this literature.</p>", "access_right": "open", "creators": [{"name": "Hoppe, Eric Christian", "affiliation": null, "orcid": "0009-0003-8011-9078"}], "keywords": ["topological data analysis", "persistent homology", "link prediction", "data leakage", "evaluation protocol", "multi-parameter persistence", "fibered barcodes"], "dates": [{"type": "created"}], "resource_type": {"title": "Preprint", "type": "publication", "subtype": "preprint"}, "license": {"id": "cc-by-4.0"}, "relations": {"version": [{"index": 0, "is_last": true, "parent": {"pid_type": "recid", "pid_value": "21181977"}}]}}, "title": "Target-Edge Leakage Inflates Topological Link Prediction: A Quantification and a Corrected Protocol", "links": {"self": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/21181978", "self_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/21181978", "preview_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/21181978?preview=1", "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21181978", "self_doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21181978", "self_doi_html": "https://zenodo.org/doi/10.5281/zenodo.21181978", "reserve_doi": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/21181978/draft/pids/doi", "parent": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/21181977", "parent_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/21181977", "parent_doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21181977", "parent_doi_html": "https://zenodo.org/doi/10.5281/zenodo.21181977", "self_iiif_manifest": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:21181978/manifest", "self_iiif_sequence": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:21181978/sequence/default", "files": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/21181978/files", "media_files": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/21181978/media-files", "thumbnails": {"10": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:21181978:Manuscript.pdf/full/%5E10,/0/default.jpg", "50":